      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To verify the diagnostic importance of the plasma lactate concentration in acute abdominal disease and to examine its role as a marker of mesenteric ischaemia."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "Prospective study."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SETTING",
          "content": "Two departments of surgery, one in Sweden and one in Germany."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SUBJECTS",
          "content": "85 patients with acute abdominal symptoms."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES",
          "content": "Correlation between the plasma lactate concentration before operation or (in the case of those not operated on) before the diagnosis was established, and the final diagnosis."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Plasma lactate concentrations exceeded the reference range in all cases of mesenteric ischaemia (n = 20) and general bacterial peritonitis (n = 15) and in half of the 20 cases of intestinal obstruction. They were also raised in 3 of 10 cases of acute pancreatitis."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "A raised plasma lactate concentration is always a sign of an acute life-threatening condition, and usually indicates the need for an emergency operation. As a marker of mesenteric ischaemia its sensitivity was 100% and its specificity 42%. We conclude that a raised serum lactate concentration is the best marker of mesenteric ischaemia to date."
        }
      ]},
